<p>Can I suggest a few grammatical/structural changes that might make the early part of the article flow better?</p>
<div class="collapsible-block">
<div class="collapsible-block-folded"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">+&nbsp;Open</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded" style="display:none">
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">-&nbsp;Close</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-content">
<p><strong>Description:</strong> SCP-5450 is a recipe known as shark fin soup, which manifests anomalous properties when produced from sharks within the Shanghai region of China. Selachians within this area, now designated SCP-5450-1, are biologically indistinguishable from normal sharks barring the presence of a pair of lungs. The anomalous property of SCP-5450 manifests upon consumption, causing the subject to transform into a species of selachian. Following transformation, the subject will find its way to a body of water via currently unknown means. A small number of cases have been observed to move their fins in a manner similar to walking. Testing has been preemptively denied by Site Director Jorge Clark as the effects of SCP-5450 are well documented and apparent.<br />
<span style="white-space: pre-wrap;">&#32;</span><br />
<strong>Discovery:</strong> SCP-5450 was discovered when several families went missing in Shanghai followed by several reports of walking sharks online. Witnesses to instances of SCP-5450-1 were promptly amnesticized. As of 06/07/2019, there are estimated to be 452 instances of SCP-5450-1, making them an endangered species.</p>
</div>
</div>
</div>
<br />
Also, they wouldn't really be an endangered species, because that's a designation usually given by a governmental organization, which the Foundation isn't.
